{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GPFBamcK1xQMuv9525msQa","type":"tool_result","content":"grep: maximum repetition exceeds 255\n----\n**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n----\n**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot would otherwise sit idle until that loop's next poll. To avoid that, a released slot (or a raised `max_pods`) wakes the loops with live demand for one tick, after a short debounce (default 2s) so a burst of releases makes one wake; those loops offer as usual, so class order and reservations still decide, and a paused controller ignores the wake. The merger, prwatch and scanners are never woken this way. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n---\n181:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, a\n245:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the \n310:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting\n405:The merger is controller code, not an agent, and takes no job slot. Every 5 minutes it merges at most one PR that has cleared every gate, oldest first, and the merge closes the issue. It also updates at most one behind branch per tick, so CI capacity goes to the next merge rather than to churn. \n540:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` i\n571:- **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct pus\n578:Start on one repo (albertcbrown/ard itself, dogfooding; decided, #6) with the local executor and a human approval gate; humans merge until Gate 2, then the merger takes over. Widen only when the numbers say the loop is trustworthy. Gate 2 is run from [docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md](site\n595:Phase 0 runs in dry-run: the controller logs every issue, label and commit it would make, and does none of them. Dry-run keeps its own state (`~/.ard/state-dryrun/`) and records would-file findings under synthetic negative issue numbers, so repeated scans don't re-propose them and `max_open` is","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ccc5aeff-cf1d-4993-8bb8-599697059519","uuid":"e8ffbb8d-3f22-4b8f-8720-2f963986b51a","timestamp":"2026-11-25T13:40:30.673Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"grep: maximum repetition exceeds 255\n----\n**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity (issues filed under `route: none` or `needs-human` lack `ard:pending` and are never candidates; #204 may widen this) for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\n----\n**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot would otherwise sit idle until that loop's next poll. To avoid that, a released slot (or a raised `max_pods`) wakes the loops